tools/precommit_check.sh
1: #!/bin/bash
2: #  PRE-COMMIT REWORK CHECK.  Run from git-bash before every commit:
3: #      bash /c/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/precommit_check.sh ["commit message file"]
4: #
5: #  WHY THIS EXISTS, measured on 2026-08-14: SEVEN OF FIFTEEN COMMITS THAT DAY
6: #  (47%) FIXED THE COMMIT IMMEDIATELY BEFORE THEM -- #407->#405, #409->#408,
7: #  #411->#410, #413->#412, #415->#414, #417->#416, #406->#404.  Raw throughput
8: #  was never the constraint; REWORK was.  Every one of those was caught later by
9: #  a gate or a seat, i.e. after the commit existed.
10: #
11: #  *** WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, said here so a green run is not read as more than it
12: #  is.  Of the three rework classes, only ONE is fully decidable by a script:
13: #    1. twin-tree propagation      -- FULLY mechanical (a cmp).  Check A.
14: #    2. a claim that outruns the diff -- PARTIALLY mechanical.  Whether a file is
15: #       named in the message but absent from the diff is decidable; whether a
16: #       SENTENCE IS TRUE is not.  Checks C and D.
17: #    3. a detector that does not test what it names -- NOT mechanical at all.
18: #       Only a mutation run settles it, and the mutant is a human choice.
19: #       Check E can only ask whether one was RECORDED.
20: #  A green run means the decidable things were checked.  It is not evidence
21: #  about correctness. ***
22: set -u
23: #  ROOT IS DERIVED FROM THIS SCRIPT'S OWN LOCATION, not hardcoded.  This file lives in
24: #  GXEMUL-SEC/tools/, so the project root is two levels up.  It used to carry an absolute
25: #  path, which is only half-tracked: a fresh clone anywhere else would have run against
26: #  whatever happened to be at the old address, or nothing.  $GXROOT overrides for testing.
27: _HERE="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}")" && pwd)"
28: ROOT="${GXROOT:-$(cd "$_HERE/../.." && pwd)}"
29: SEC=$ROOT/GXEMUL-SEC
30: EST=$ROOT/est
31: BLD=$ROOT/build
32: MSGFILE=${1:-}
33: fail=0
34: warn=0
35: 
36: say  () { printf \'%s\\n\' "$*"; }
37: bad  () { fail=$((fail+1)); printf \'  FAIL  %s\\n\' "$*"; }
38: soft () { warn=$((warn+1)); printf \'  WARN  %s\\n\' "$*"; }
39: good () { printf \'  ok    %s\\n\' "$*"; }
40: #  `warn` is called at the four "checker MISSING" branches and was DEFINED NOWHERE -- so a
41: #  missing pipeline checker printed `warn: command not found` on stderr and its gate was
42: #  silently disabled, which is the exact failure those branches exist to announce.  Measured
43: #  by renaming check_stage_panels.py and check_seats_read.py away: both gates vanished and the
44: #  run still said PRECOMMIT_PASS.
45: warn () { soft "$@"; }
46: 
47: cd "$SEC" || { say "no repo at $SEC"; exit 9; }
48: # ---------------------------------------------------------------- F
49: #  THE PASS-2 RECEIPT, AND THIS IS THE SECOND VERSION BECAUSE THE FIRST COULD NOT FIRE.
50: #
51: #  R6 shipped without a pass-2 review of its diff.  That was caught externally,
52: #  recorded, and corrected -- and R7 then did exactly the same thing one round
53: #  later.  So a mechanical check was written.  A pass-2 seat then MEASURED that
54: #  the check itself was vacuous: it sat below the "nothing staged" early exit, so
55: #  on a clean post-commit tree -- the only state in which a missed pass 2 is
56: #  actually detectable -- the script returned before reaching it.  It also keyed
57: #  on source mtime, which any checkout or copy moves, and accepted any panel
58: #  directory as evidence without asking what that panel reviewed.
59: #
60: #  A check that cannot fail is worse than no check, because it reassures.  This
61: #  version fixes all four faults:
62: #    * it runs BEFORE the early exit, so a clean tree still gets checked;
63: #    * it asks about the PREVIOUS src-touching COMMIT, not the working tree --
64: #      you cannot be required to have reviewed the commit you are about to make,
65: #      but you can be required to have reviewed the last one;
regress/artifacts/dashokclass_20261016/staged/tools/pipeline/check_entry_domain.py
1: #!/usr/bin/env python3
2: """A NEW LEDGER ANSWER CARRIES A `d` THE DASHBOARD CLASSIFIES -- OR NONE.  2026-10-16, ledger `dashokclass`.
3: 
4: THE DOMAIN is the four glyphs gen_dashboard.py's D_CLASS maps -- \u2713 approved, \u2715 objection, \u23f8 held, \u2026 no answer --
5: or no `d` at all (the renderer's default is \u2026).  MEASURED when this landed: 1,397 ledger entries carried an explicit
6: `d` OUTSIDE the four -- OK 439, other 399, N/A 253, dates 128, y 89, HELD 31, blank 29, NOT-FIRED 22, WALL 7 (Fable
7: 5.1, regress/artifacts/adj_20261016/fable5/meas_C/okdomain_census.txt) -- and until the same commit every one of them
8: that reached a cell rendered in the SEAT-FAILURE class, because the renderer's fallback was `pend`.  The renderer now
9: shows them as one residue class, "legacy -- verdict unclassified" (an explicit `d` it cannot classify is neither
10: approval nor a seat failure); this check stops that population GROWING.  Ruled by Fable 5.1, GPT-6 Astra and Grok 4.7
11: (regress/artifacts/adj_20261016/RULINGS_C_records.md, F2): every mechanical sweep of the old entries is withdrawn --
12: an OK becomes \u2713 or anything else only when a reader rewrites that one entry.
13: 
14: ASTRA'S FOUR COMPLETENESS CONDITIONS (adj_20261016/astra/C_records/codex_adjC.txt), each held here:
15:   1. a MISSING `d` passes -- it renders as \u2026 ;
16:   2. an entry that is not an ANSWER passes whatever its `d` -- holds, annotations, records (stage_tokens.is_answer);
17:   3. the cutoff is INCLUSIVE, and the controls test entries dated before it, on it and after it;
18:   4. the controls carry a POSITIVE row (a valid glyph after the cutoff passes) beside the negatives and a mutant with
19:      the baseline removed.
20: 
21: GRANDFATHERING IS AGAINST THE EXISTING ENTRIES, NOT AGAINST A DATE (the ruling's amendment: a date-only cutoff cannot
22: stop a back-dated or an undated new entry).  An off-domain answer passes only if it is dated BEFORE `CUTOFF` AND its
23: (row, phase, seat, date, d) tuple is within the count frozen in `entry_domain_baseline.json` -- the off-domain answers
24: present when this check landed, written once by regress/artifacts/dashokclass_20261016/write_baseline.py and never by
25: this file.  So a back-dated or undated addition exceeds its tuple's frozen count and fails, and so does any off-domain
26: answer dated on or after CUTOFF.  Rewriting a grandfathered entry's `d` to a glyph only shrinks the population.
27: 
28: Usage: check_entry_domain.py          (GXLEDGER overrides the ledger path; GXDOMAIN_BASELINE the baseline's)
29: Exit: 0 clean; 1 a new off-domain answer (each named); 2 apparatus failure (baseline or ledger unreadable)."""
30: import collections, io, json, os, sys
31: 
32: HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
33: sys.path.insert(0, HERE)
34: import stage_tokens as st  # noqa: E402
35: 
36: LEDGER = os.environ.get("GXLEDGER") or os.path.join(HERE, "ledger.json")
37: BASELINE = os.environ.get("GXDOMAIN_BASELINE") or os.path.join(HERE, "entry_domain_baseline.json")
38: DOMAIN = ("\u2713", "\u2715", "\u23f8", "\u2026")      # gen_dashboard.py\'s D_CLASS keys; the two must agree
39: CUTOFF = "2026-10-17"               # inclusive: an off-domain answer dated on or after it always fails
40: 
41: 
42: def off_domain_answers(ledger):
43:     """(row, phase, seat, date, d) for every ANSWER entry carrying an explicit `d` outside DOMAIN."""
44:     out = []
45:     for r in ledger.get("rows", []):
46:         for x in r.get("entries", []):
47:             if "d" not in x or x["d"] in DOMAIN or not st.is_answer(x):
48:                 continue
49:             out.append((r.get("id"), x.get("phase") or "", x.get("seat") or "", x.get("date") or "", str(x["d"])))
50:     return out
51: 
52: 
53: def main():
54:     try:
55:         ledger = json.load(io.open(LEDGER, encoding="utf-8"))
56:     except Exception as ex:
57:         print("APPARATUS FAIL: ledger unreadable: %s (%s)" % (LEDGER, ex)); return 2
58:     try:
59:         base = collections.Counter({tuple(k[:5]): k[5] for k in json.load(io.open(BASELINE, encoding="utf-8"))})
60:     except Exception as ex:
61:         print("APPARATUS FAIL: baseline unreadable: %s (%s)" % (BASELINE, ex)); return 2
62:     cur = off_domain_answers(ledger)
63:     seen = collections.Counter()
64:     bad = []
65:     for t in cur:
66:         seen[t] += 1
67:         if t[3] >= CUTOFF:
68:             bad.append((t, "dated on or after the cutoff %s" % CUTOFF))
69:         elif seen[t] > base[t]:
70:             bad.append((t, "not among the %d grandfathered entries of this tuple" % base[t]))
71:     print("entry domain: %d off-domain answer entries; %d grandfathered (baseline %d); cutoff %s inclusive"
72:           % (len(cur), len(cur) - len(bad), sum(base.values()), CUTOFF))
73:     for (row, phase, seat, date, d), why in bad:
74:         print("  NEW OFF-DOMAIN ANSWER  row %s  phase %s  seat %s  date %s  d %r -- %s"
75:               % (row, phase, seat, date or "(none)", d, why))
76:     if bad:
77:         print("  -> give each a `d` from %s (or omit `d`, which renders as \u2026); a non-answer record is exempt"
78:               % " ".join(DOMAIN))
79:         return 1
80:     return 0
81: 
82: 
83: if __name__ == "__main__":
84:     sys.exit(main())

